<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mariano Gálvez</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1794">1794</a>–<a href="/years/1862">1862</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mariano Gálvez was a Guatemalan lawyer and liberal politician, head of state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> within the Central American Federation from 1831 to 1838. He introduced civil marriage, the Livingston penal codes, secular education and land grants to foreign colonists. A cholera epidemic and a head tax provoked the rural revolt led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a> that brought him down.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mariano-galvez.jpg" alt="Mariano Gálvez" width="199" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mariano_G%C3%A1lvez.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
